WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.680
Surprising cameos, hidden song&nbsp;
meanings and a makeup tutorial?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.120
Did you catch all these details in&nbsp;
Baz Luhrmann's HBO Elvis biopic?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.400
Fans may have missed all the hidden Star Trek&nbsp;
references throughout the film. First, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.720
beginning of the movie, at the International&nbsp;
Hotel in Vegas, there's an advertisement for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.820
the Star Trek Experience on the sign out front.&nbsp;
And there's at least one other reference later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.340
in the film during the Christmas special. While&nbsp;
Tom Parker and the NBC studio executives walk the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.380
hallways at NBC studios, there are several Star&nbsp;
Trek posters hanging up that promote the show.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.400
Some might think these small Star&nbsp;
Trek details are merely a coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.620
but they are actually purposely hidden in the&nbsp;
film. The King was a huge fan of Star Trek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.680
of both the cast and the show's storylines.&nbsp;
Celeste Yarnall, Star Trek actress and Elvis'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.440
on-screen costar, talked about how much Elvis&nbsp;
loved the show, and that Elvis even named one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.480
of his horses "Star Trek." Because of the King's&nbsp;
love for the show, Luhrmann wanted to include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.360
some small Star Trek details that hardcore Elvis&nbsp;
fans would recognize as an homage to his fandom.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:13.260
One of Elvis' most famous films is Blue Hawaii.&nbsp;
In the film, Elvis plays Chad, a boy who is meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.940
to take over his family business, but throws&nbsp;
it away in favor of working for his girlfriend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:23.760
as a tour guide at the agency she owns. The&nbsp;
film also starred the late Angela Lansbury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.580
and included hits like "Blue Hawaii," "Aloha&nbsp;
Oe," and, most famously, "Can't Help Falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.260
In Love." There's a whole section of Luhrmann's&nbsp;
film that pays tribute to Elvis' movie career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.000
but one of the homages to Elvis' acting&nbsp;
is a small detail you might have missed.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.180
"I'm gonna show you what the&nbsp;
real Elvis is like tonight."

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:48.120
A bit over 35 minutes into Elvis, he and B.B.&nbsp;
King go shopping for new outfits at a store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.980
called Lansky's. While in the store, there's a&nbsp;
shot of all the shelves and other products in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:57.960
the store aside from what the two musicians are&nbsp;
purchasing. In this shot, you can see a row of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.240
shirts displayed behind Elvis, and one of the&nbsp;
shirts is the iconic red Hawaiian-style shirt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.760
he wears in Blue Hawaii. It's a small detail and&nbsp;
the shot is fleeting, but diehard Elvis fans can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:13.980
keep their eyes peeled for this small homage&nbsp;
to one of Elvis' most famous acting moments.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.860
One of the most action-packed sequences of the&nbsp;
film comes from the Viva Las Vegas montage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.960
where the audience sees shots of real&nbsp;
Elvis films, reenactments featuring Butler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.540
and moments with Elvis and Priscilla or Elvis&nbsp;
and his "Memphis Mafia," as they were called.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.000
There are several panels going at once, making it&nbsp;
the perfect place to slip in some smaller details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.680
about the musician and actor. Though many fans&nbsp;
see this as a moment where it's once again hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.600
to tell the difference between the real Elvis and&nbsp;
Butler's iteration, there's one panel where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.760
can absolutely be sure it's the real Elvis because&nbsp;
of the other actor making a cameo in the scene.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.660
One of the panels behind Butler's Elvis&nbsp;
while he's filming the surfing shots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.920
shows a memorable scene from Elvis'&nbsp;
film It Happened At The World's Fair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.000
The scene involves Elvis asking a&nbsp;
young child to kick him in the shin.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.760
"No kiddin'. I'll tell you what.&nbsp;
You kick me in the shin real hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.720
I'll give you a quarter.&nbsp;
Okay, go ahead, play it. Ahh!"

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.980
The child actor is actually Kurt Russell,&nbsp;
who's starred in films like The Hateful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.240
Eight and Guardians of the Galaxy&nbsp;
Vol. 2. The Russell cameo is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.520
measningful because he eventually played&nbsp;
Elvis in John Carpenter's 1979 biopic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.100
Elvis. The 2022 Kurt Russell moment is brief,&nbsp;
but there for those who pay close attention.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.120
Elvis experienced a lot of mental&nbsp;
stress throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.280
mostly because Tom Parker kept him continually&nbsp;
running with no room to rest. This type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.360
exhaustion is something many celebrities&nbsp;
experience, but Elvis, in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.020
was dealing with the assassinations&nbsp;
of John F. Kennedy and Martin Luther&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:50.460
King Jr., while watching other celebrities&nbsp;
murdered or attacked just for their fame.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.100
"Memphis is burning. …And to fear his brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:59.040
When you teach that he's a lesser man&nbsp;
because of his color or his beliefs."

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.860
This took a huge toll on him and inspired paranoia&nbsp;
in Elvis that helped Parker control his life.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:10.200
One scene that perfectly encapsulates this is&nbsp;
when Elvis is in the hospital for exhaustion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.440
Parker is reading a newspaper with Sharon Tate's&nbsp;
murder blazed across the cover. This is used as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.720
plot device to spark a discussion about fear for&nbsp;
the lives of entertainers. But for Butler fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.160
it can be seen as an homage to his&nbsp;
role as Tex, one of Tate's killers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.660
in the Quentin Tarantino film Once Upon&nbsp;
a Time in Hollywood. It's a small detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.232
that only fans of Butler or the Tarantino&nbsp;
film would know, but it's fun nonetheless.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.660
The relationship between Elvis and his&nbsp;
wife, Priscilla, is one of the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.580
primary storylines. The audience gets to see&nbsp;
their romance at every stage, starting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.760
how they meet and bond – 
 
"I've never met anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.800
like you."
 
"Well, I hope not."

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.720
– and ending with their divorce and co-parenting&nbsp;
style. There's one scene that's particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.620
heartbreaking because of how clear it is to&nbsp;
the audience that they still love each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.180
but just can't work as a couple anymore.&nbsp;
When they are talking together in the car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.500
toward the end of the film, it's a heart to&nbsp;
heart that only Priscilla could help Elvis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:12.360
get through. They both get out of the car to&nbsp;
leave, and Elvis looks at Priscilla and mouths,

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.500
"I will always love you."

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:22.080
It's a tearful moment, but it's one with a deeper&nbsp;
meaning than just the sentiment. Elvis famously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.580
sang Dolly Parton's "I Will Always Love You" to&nbsp;
Priscilla when the two were getting divorced.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.320
"His wife told me that, when they divorced, that&nbsp;
when they were coming out of the courthouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.220
Elvis sang that song to her and&nbsp;
that just touched me so deep."

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.600
But even more than this, Elvis wanted&nbsp;
to professionally record Parton's song,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.640
and she wanted that as well. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.370
Parker wanted to take half of the publishing&nbsp;
rights for the song, and Parton refused.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.580
"It broke my heart. I cried all night, you know,&nbsp;
'cause I was not gonna hear Elvis sing it."

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.709
So Elvis never recorded it.

00:05:52.709 --> 00:05:57.780
The catalyst for Parker and Elvis' relationship&nbsp;
throughout the film starts with their intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:03.540
conversation at the carnival. Parker notices&nbsp;
Elvis standing alone, and recognizes him as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.460
lost outcast who would be easily influenced&nbsp;
by his management. He approaches Elvis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.660
but Elvis is suddenly called away by his&nbsp;
friends to go into the house of mirrors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:18.180
delaying the conversation between the musician and&nbsp;
manager. However, when Parker first notices Elvis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:23.700
he notices something else, too: the advertisement&nbsp;
for "The Geek" posted behind his view of Elvis.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:30.000
This hidden detail has a deeper meaning. First,&nbsp;
a "geek" is known to carnival lovers as the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.100
grotesque or shocking of all the acts. These&nbsp;
performers bite the heads off animals or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:41.100
eat them alive. This slightly references 1947's&nbsp;
Nightmare Alley, which was Parker's favorite film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.720
so that's one meaning to it. However,&nbsp;
it also references the fact that Parker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.520
saw how audiences reacted to Elvis' act, and&nbsp;
how they couldn't control the pleasure they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.420
felt from it but weren't sure if they were&nbsp;
supposed to be enjoying it or rejecting it.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:59.880
That goes for most of the world's response to the&nbsp;
musician, actually. Parker knew Elvis could be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.800
music industry's "geek" act, and took advantage&nbsp;
of that in his management of the musician.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.060
One of the biggest parts of the&nbsp;
film is Elvis' comeback special,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.580
which is where you really see Elvis defy Parker&nbsp;
to create what he wants his audience to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:19.500
not what Parker is pressuring him to do.
 
"I don't give a damn what the Colonel thinks."

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.600
Elvis fans will watch the film's version of&nbsp;
the special and note that it is recreated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:30.120
nearly perfectly by Luhrmann. Every detail of&nbsp;
it is so precise, and Butler's performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.920
though excellent throughout the whole film,&nbsp;
is especially well done here. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:41.100
one detail that was particularly accurate to&nbsp;
add is extremely subtle, but tells a huge story.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:46.980
In the scene, when Butler's Elvis first starts&nbsp;
singing, he reaches for the microphone. When he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:51.840
does this, his hand shakes slightly, like&nbsp;
he's nervous to perform. This is because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.640
as Elvis fans will note, in the original&nbsp;
comeback special, Elvis hadn't performed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.080
in front of a live audience for a long time&nbsp;
and was nervous. This caused his hand to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:06.060
shake as he was getting into the feeling of the&nbsp;
performance, and Butler included this detail in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.340
the film version to make it more authentic&nbsp;
and exact to Elvis' behavior at the special.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:16.380
One of the defining moments of Elvis' life and&nbsp;
in the film comes from Martin Luther King Jr.'s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.780
death. His assassination deeply impacted Elvis,&nbsp;
and eventually sparked his rebellion against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.700
Parker's agenda and deciding to work with Steve&nbsp;
Binder to create the special he wanted to make.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.980
"Dr. King was shot eight miles from Graceland&nbsp;
while I was out here singing to turtles."

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.200
However, there's a hidden detwail before this,&nbsp;
while Elvis is contemplating King Jr.'s death.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.220
In the scene, Priscilla walks into&nbsp;
Elvis' trailer to find him watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:46.140
Dr. King's memorial. She greets him by saying&nbsp;
"Satnin," which some might take to be a pet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.400
name or not really notice she says it at&nbsp;
all. This was no throwaway line, though.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:56.280
"Satnin" is actually what Elvis used to call&nbsp;
his mother, Gladys Presley. He used this word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.200
because of her satin-smooth skin. It was more&nbsp;
than that, though, because to the two of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.940
this word was a form of affection. Elvis and&nbsp;
his mother had an extremely close relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:10.800
right up until her death. It was slightly&nbsp;
strained by the influence of Tom Parker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:16.020
but was still pure and lovely. Priscilla&nbsp;
greeting Elvis in this way is not only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:20.280
nice way to squeeze that reference into the&nbsp;
film, but also especially meaningful because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.734
he's grappling with Dr. King's death and it likely&nbsp;
brings up feelings about his own mother's death.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:32.220
Another small detail comes once more with the Viva&nbsp;
Las Vegas Hollywood movie montage. There's a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:37.560
in the top left corner of the five panel layout&nbsp;
where Elvis is seen doing Priscilla's makeup. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.540
might seem like a small piece of romance between&nbsp;
the two, but is really a detail that points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:48.000
toward a larger circumstance between Elvis and&nbsp;
Priscilla. Most people might not know that Elvis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.980
was very particular about Priscilla's appearance,&nbsp;
and actually taught her how to do her makeup.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:57.540
In Priscilla's memoir, Elvis and Me,&nbsp;
she talked about how Elvis created her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:02.460
and how she didn't really know who she&nbsp;
was as a person without Elvis. She wrote,

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:20.160
Elvis had huge control issues when it&nbsp;
came to Priscilla. But she didn't take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.020
it as a negative. She saw it as more of a&nbsp;
guidance to creating who she was. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:28.920
with no separation between what she&nbsp;
wanted and what Elvis wanted for her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.560
it became hard for her to see&nbsp;
herself as her own person.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.500
One element of Elvis that fans enjoy is&nbsp;
the mixing of the old Elvis tracks with new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:42.840
artists' music. Artists like Doja Cat teamed&nbsp;
up on the Elvis and Big Mama Thornton hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.700
"Hound Dog" to create a new spin on the&nbsp;
classics. Another mashup audiences enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:52.020
was the Elvis song "Viva Las Vegas"&nbsp;
mixed with Britney Spears' "Toxic."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:56.820
It circulated around TikTok and, because it&nbsp;
wasn't released on the film's soundtrack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:02.460
fans begged for the cut to be released. Though&nbsp;
this song is an absolute hit, it also serves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:06.960
as an important detail in the film for those&nbsp;
wanting to do more of a deep dive on its meaning.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:13.080
One of the biggest news items of 2021 and 2022&nbsp;
came from the movement to free popular musician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.100
Britney Spears from her conservatorship.&nbsp;
She was poorly managed by her family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:22.380
particularly her father, and suffered a lot&nbsp;
of abuse in the process. Because this song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:27.180
plays while Elvis is being manipulated in&nbsp;
his acting career by Parker, it's clearly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:31.800
reference to how both artists were exploited for&nbsp;
the profits of those who handled their affairs.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:38.940
"Now you listen to me. The only thing that matters&nbsp;
is that that man gets up on that stage tonight."

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:42.360
It's a small detail that people&nbsp;
might not immediately put together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:47.820
but, as Bustle writes, it's a way to&nbsp;
link two of history's iconic musicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:50.340
through some of their shared negative experiences.

